General elections were held in [[Bolivia]] on 17 August 2025 to elect the [[President of Bolivia|president]], [[Vice President of Bolivia|vice president]], and all members of the [[Plurinational Legislative Assembly]]. Incumbent president [[Luis Arce]] of the ruling [[Movimiento al Socialismo]] (MAS-IPSP) did not seek reelection, amid internal party divisions and widespread dissatisfaction over shortages of essential goods. No candidate secured a majority in the presidential election, leading to a [[Two-round system|runoff]] on 19 October 2025 between Senator [[Rodrigo Paz]] of the [[Christian Democratic Party (Bolivia)|Christian Democratic Party]] (PDC) and former president [[Jorge Quiroga]] of [[Libre (Bolivia)|Libre]]. Paz won with 55.0% of the vote, marking the first time in Bolivian history that the presidency changed hands through a runoff election and ending two decades of MAS dominance. Meanwhile, MAS retained only two seats in the [[Chamber of Deputies (Bolivia)|Chamber of Deputies]] while losing all seats in the [[Chamber of Senators (Bolivia)|Senate]].<ref name="ap-20250818">{{cite news |last=DeBre |first=Isabel |date=18 August 2025 |title=Bolivia heads to a presidential runoff as 2 decades of left-wing dominance ends |url=https://apnews.com/article/bolivia-elections-right-wing-274e22ad9f493f60669a01615d3fef65 |access-date=18 August 2025 |work=AP News}}</ref>

==Background==
{{see also|2024 Bolivian protests}}
The election was held amid a schism within the ruling [[Movimiento al Socialismo]] (MAS) between incumbent president [[Luis Arce]] and former president [[Evo Morales]]. The former allies fell out after Arce's [[2020 Bolivian general election|election in 2020]], when Morales—returning from exile following the [[2019 Bolivian political crisis|political unrest]] triggered by his [[2019 Bolivian general election|contested reelection bid]]—sought to regain control of the party.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Meakem |first=Allison |date=2 January 2025 |title=Elections to Watch in 2025 |url=https://foreignpolicy.com/2025/01/02/top-world-elections-2025-canada-germany-ecuador-australia-poland-bolivia/ |access-date=7 April 2025 |website=Foreign Policy}}</ref> Public dissatisfaction with the Arce government was further spurred by shortages of essential goods like gasoline, diesel, food, and medicine in late 2024 and early 2025.<ref>{{Cite web |date=28 November 2024 |title='Everything is expensive!' Bolivia faces a shocking economic collapse |url=https://apnews.com/article/bolivia-fuel-crisis-economy-arce-president-gas-b43cff9dcfebdca40d35aad6911f055f |access-date=19 May 2025 |website=AP News|first1=Paola|last1=Flores|first2=Isabel|last2=DeBre}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Bueno |first=Boris |date=19 May 2025 |title=Agenda informativa: ocho organizaciones políticas inscribirán a sus binomios y a las planchas legislativas; Evo Morales también anuncia su registro |url=https://eju.tv/2025/05/agenda-informativa-ocho-organizaciones-politicas-inscribiran-a-sus-binomios-y-a-las-planchas-legislativas-evo-morales-tambien-anuncia-su-registro/ |access-date=19 May 2025 |website=eju.tv |language=es}}</ref>

Morales announced his intention to run as the candidate of the Front for Victory (FPV), despite a ban from the [[Plurinational Constitutional Tribunal]] on his running for president again.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bueno |first=Boris |date=15 March 2025 |title=Doria Medina y Tuto Quiroga aceleran la conformación de alianzas para sostener su probable candidatura por el bloque de unidad |url=https://eju.tv/2025/03/doria-medina-y-tuto-quiroga-aceleran-la-conformacion-de-alianzas-para-sostener-su-probable-candidatura-por-el-bloque-de-unidad/ |access-date=18 March 2025 |website=EJU |language=es}}</ref> The government subsequently deregistered the FPV, citing its failure to meet the mandatory 3% threshold in the previous election.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bueno |first=Boris |date=19 May 2025 |title=Un Evo desesperado advierte ante la casi inminente inhabilitación de su candidatura |url=https://eju.tv/2025/05/un-evo-desesperado-advierte-ante-la-casi-inminente-inhabilitacion-de-su-candidatura/ |access-date=19 May 2025 |website=eju.tv |language=es}}</ref> In reaction to being barred from the race, Morales called for a boycott of the vote. From a rural compound guarded by supporters,<ref>{{cite web |last1=Safi |first1=Michael |last2=Rogero |first2=Tiago |last3=McDonagh |first3=George |last4=Block |first4=Eli |last5=Manley |first5=Ivor |last6=Yusuf |first6=Courtney |title=From president to fugitive: in the jungle hideout of Evo Morales |work=The Guardian |date=15 May 2025 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/news/audio/2025/may/15/from-president-to-fugitive-in-the-jungle-hideout-of-evo-morales |access-date=17 August 2025}}</ref> he promised to mobilize his supporters and "give battle on the streets" if a right-wing candidate won.<ref>{{cite web |title=Fears of unrest surge as Bolivia readies for its first democratic handover in over two decades |url=https://latinamericareports.com/fears-of-unrest-surge-as-bolivia-readies-for-its-first-democratic-handover-in-over-two-decades/12114/|work=Latin America Reports|first=Mark|last=Krukov|date=17 August 2025 |access-date=24 August 2025}}</ref> Right-wing figures like [[Jorge Quiroga]] vowed to arrest Morales if they won, leading rural [[Cocalero|coca unions]] such as the Six Federations to pledge a guerrilla war in his defense.<ref>{{cite news |title=Bolivia's crazy kingdom of coca |newspaper=The Economist |url=https://www.economist.com/the-americas/2025/08/14/bolivias-crazy-kingdom-of-coca |date=14 August 2025 |access-date=17 August 2025 |quote=The Six Federations is preparing to resist. María Eugenia Ledezma, its top female leader until a few months ago, says they will use guerrilla tactics against soldiers who venture into the Chapare, depriving them of sleep, then attacking with sticks and stones. She says miners have been teaching people how to make boobytraps with dynamite; sympathisers in the army have been training the young. 'Many of us, many leaders, will surely die or be imprisoned,' she says, grim-faced.}}</ref>

On 14 May 2025, President Arce announced he would not seek a second term.<ref name=":2" /><ref>{{Cite web |date=14 May 2025 |title=Presidente boliviano Luis Arce anuncia que no buscará la reelección en comicios de agosto |url=https://apnews.com/article/bolivia-arce-elecciones-candidatura-morales-9c68d5a46f126da9865cdf1a2af041bc |access-date=14 May 2025 |website=AP News |language=es}}</ref> The MAS nominated government minister [[Eduardo Del Castillo]] in his place. Two days later, on 16 May, demonstrators attempting to register Morales as a candidate clashed with police in [[La Paz]].<ref name=":3">{{Cite web |date=17 May 2025 |title=Supporters of Bolivia's ex-leader Morales clash with police in push to secure his candidacy |url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/05/17/americas/bolivia-morales-supporters-clash-police-intl-hnk |access-date=17 May 2025 |website=CNN}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=17 May 2025 |title=Bolivia police teargas Morales supporters protesting electoral ban |url=https://www.straitstimes.com/world/bolivia-police-teargas-morales-supporters-protesting-electoral-ban |access-date=17 May 2025 |work=The Straits Times |issn=0585-3923}}</ref>

Meanwhile, right-leaning parties formed a Unity Bloc to oppose the MAS; its candidates included [[Samuel Doria Medina]] and Jorge Quiroga. Several candidates, including Quiroga and [[Chi Hyun Chung]], registered their campaigns using minor party labels, such as the [[Revolutionary Left Front (Bolivia)|Revolutionary Left Front]] (FRI) and the [[Revolutionary Nationalist Movement]] (MNR), despite the historical ideological differences between these parties and the candidates.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Molina |first=Fernando |date=28 April 2025 |title=Guerra electoral en Bolivia: partidos 'en préstamo', 13 candidatos y el resultado más incierto en 20 años |url=https://elpais.com/america/2025-04-28/guerra-electoral-en-bolivia-partidos-en-prestamo-13-candidatos-y-el-resultado-mas-incierto-en-20-anos.html |access-date=15 May 2025 |website=El País América |language=es}}</ref>

==Electoral system==
{{Main|Elections in Bolivia}}

Approximately 7.9 million people were eligible to vote in the 2025 election.<ref name="apelection">{{Cite web |date=15 August 2025 |title=Bolivian voters are hungry for change — and disillusioned by the options ahead of election |url=https://apnews.com/article/bolivia-election-morales-arce-president-youth-trump-95036a915013c06c6f783899b320dc13 |access-date=15 August 2025 |website=AP News |first1=Isabel |last1=DeBre |first2=Carlos|last2=Valdez}}</ref>

The President is elected through a modified [[two-round system]]. A candidate wins outright in the first round by receiving either more than 50% of the vote, or at least 40% of the vote and a lead of 10 percentage points over the nearest rival.<ref name="BBC50055725">{{Cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-50055725|title=Will Bolivians give Evo Morales a fourth term?|date=20 October 2019|work=BBC|access-date=21 October 2019|language=en-GB|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191020151045/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-50055725|archive-date=20 October 2019|url-status=live}}</ref> If neither condition is met, a run-off is held between the top two candidates.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.eldeber.com.bo/bolivia/El-Tribunal-Electoral-define-la-eventual-segunda-vuelta-para-el-15-de-diciembre-20190329-0103.html|title=El Tribunal Electoral define la eventual segunda vuelta para el 15 de diciembre|website=El Deber|access-date=2 April 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190402132651/https://www.eldeber.com.bo/bolivia/El-Tribunal-Electoral-define-la-eventual-segunda-vuelta-para-el-15-de-diciembre-20190329-0103.html|archive-date=2 April 2019|url-status=live}}</ref>

The 130 members of the [[Chamber of Deputies of Bolivia|Chamber of Deputies]] are elected through a [[mixed-member proportional representation]] (MMP) system with two votes:

* 63 deputies are elected by [[First-past-the-post voting|first-past-the-post]] in single-member districts.{{cn|date=October 2025}}
* 60 deputies are elected by [[party-list proportional representation]] from closed lists on a departmental basis, using a 3% electoral threshold.<ref name="RegimenElectoral2">{{cite web |title=Bolivia: Ley del Régimen Electoral, 30 de junio de 2010 |url=http://www.lexivox.org/norms/BO-L-N26.xhtml |access-date=10 February 2015 |website=Lexivox}}</ref> These list seats are allocated to parties proportionally based on the presidential vote, subtracting the seats they won in single-member districts to ensure overall proportionality.
* 7 deputies are reserved for indigenous representatives and elected by ''[[usos y costumbres]]'' (customary law). A voter may only vote in one type of constituency ([[Coexistence (electoral systems)|coexistence]]).<ref name="RegimenElectoral">{{cite web|title=Bolivia: Ley del Régimen Electoral, 30 de junio de 2010|url=http://www.lexivox.org/norms/BO-L-N26.xhtml|website=Lexivox|access-date=8 October 2020}}</ref>
* Electoral rules mandate gender parity: party lists must alternate between men and women. For single-member districts, a male candidate must have a female alternate, and vice versa, ensuring that women hold at least 50% of these seats.{{cn|date=October 2025}}

The 36 members of the [[Senate of Bolivia|Chamber of Senators]]—four from each of the nine departments—are elected from closed party lists using the [[D'Hondt method]].<ref name="RegimenElectoral" /> Senate seats are also awarded based on the presidential vote.

This election uses a form of the [[double simultaneous vote]], meaning a single vote is used to elect the President (first round), Deputies, and Senators simultaneously. Consequently, voters cannot [[Split-ticket voting|split their ticket]] between the executive and legislative branches, though they can vote for a different party in the single-member deputy district elections.{{cn|date=October 2025}}

Voting is compulsory for all citizens over 18. Voters receive a card as proof of participation, which is required to withdraw one's salary from a bank for three months following the election.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.thenews.com.pk/print/343889-voting-is-compulsory-in-some-countries|title=Voting is compulsory in some countries|date=20 July 2018}}</ref> This compulsory voting is linked to a relatively high rate of invalid ballots,<ref>{{cite journal|title=Politically Unengaged, Distrusting, and Disaffected Individuals Drive the Link Between Compulsory Voting and Invalid Balloting|url=https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/political-science-research-and-methods/article/politically-unengaged-distrusting-and-disaffected-individuals-drive-the-link-between-compulsory-voting-and-invalid-balloting/9DDF631F6ADDE16907A75C37B7FC3AFE|journal=Political Science Research and Methods|first=Shane P. |last=Singh|volume=7|issue=1|date=January 2019|pages=107–123|doi=10.1017/psrm.2017.11|s2cid=157099704|url-access=subscription}}</ref> a factor often reflected in poll percentages for "would not vote."

