==Campaign==
The campaign was marked by the first televised debate in 20 years, following a ban imposed during Evo Morales's presidency. The debates featured sharp exchanges, including Samuel Doria Medina's accusations that Eduardo del Castillo had links to drug traffickers and del Castillo's criticism of Doria Medina's past electoral failures. Andrónico Rodríguez and Jorge Quiroga also clashed over alleged involvement in extrajudicial killings.<ref name="apelection"/>

=== Candidate platforms ===
The right-leaning candidates, Samuel Doria Medina and Jorge "Tuto" Quiroga, presented similar platforms focused on economic liberalization and a foreign policy re-alignment. Both pledged to:

*Arrest former president Evo Morales.<ref name="f24">{{Cite web |date=14 August 2025 |title=From socialism to shock therapy? Bolivia's presidential hopefuls make last push for votes |url=https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20250814-bolivia-presidential-hopefuls-make-last-push-for-votes |access-date=15 August 2025 |website=France 24}}</ref><ref name="guardian">{{Cite web |date=15 August 2025 |title=Bolivia election could see lurch to right as once-dominant party's time runs out |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/aug/15/bolivia-presidential-election-right-wing |access-date=15 August 2025 |website=The Guardian|first=Tiago|last=Rogero}}</ref>
*Restore diplomatic relations with the United States and Israel.<ref name="reuters">{{Cite web |date=18 August 2025 |title=Who are Bolivia's presidential runoff contenders promising economic overhaul? |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/who-are-bolivias-presidential-runoff-contenders-promising-economic-overhaul-2025-08-18/ |access-date=18 August 2025 |website=Reuters}}</ref>
*Cut public spending and eliminate fuel subsidies.<ref name="apknow">{{Cite web |date=12 August 2025 |title=Bolivia's election may spell the end of its long-ruling left. Here's what to know |url=https://apnews.com/article/bolivia-election-mas-socialism-morales-df3b502d552e5b995d082dbdbb226c1d |access-date=15 August 2025 |website=AP News}}</ref><ref name="guardian"/>
*Partially reverse the nationalizations under Morales and attract foreign investment, particularly for lithium mining.<ref name="f24"/><ref name="apknow"/>
*Dismantle inefficient state-owned companies.<ref name="apknow"/>

Doria Medina specifically promised to resolve fuel shortages within his first 100 days, a goal reflected in his campaign slogan, "100 days, dammit!"—a phrase he used after surviving a 2005 plane crash.<ref name="guardian"/><ref>{{Cite web |date=31 July 2025 |title=Meet the multimillionaire who wants to be Bolivia's first right-wing president elected in 20 years |url=https://apnews.com/article/bolivia-president-samuel-doria-medina-morales-elections-trump-49faa8947c89a6f363b907a70915abc6 |access-date=15 August 2025 |website=AP News}}</ref> He also pledged to maintain anti-poverty programs.<ref>{{Cite web |date=17 August 2025 |title=Bolivian right eyes return in elections marked by economic crisis |url=https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20250817-bolivian-right-eyes-return-in-elections-marked-by-economic-crisis |access-date=17 August 2025 |website=France 24}}</ref>

Quiroga proposed a more radical economic overhaul, praising Argentine president [[Javier Milei]]'s libertarian policies. His proposals included:

*Closing the [[Central Bank of Bolivia]], which he accused of being a "credit card" for the Arce administration.<ref name="apknow"/>
*Establishing a "popular property title" valued at $1,500 for every adult Bolivian to use as loan collateral.<ref name="reuters"/>
*Seeking a $12 billion bailout from the [[International Monetary Fund]].<ref name="reuters"/>
*A "liberal ownership revolution" to replace collective Indigenous land titles with individual, inheritable rights.<ref name="auto">{{Cite web |date=26 August 2025 |title=Bolivia's right-wing ex-president Jorge Quiroga says he'll 'change everything' if elected |url=https://apnews.com/article/bolivia-tuto-quiroga-rightwing-morales-us-elections-18be23a5a62d6f6870e274baeb1c8b97 |access-date=26 August 2025 |website=AP News}}</ref>
*Canceling lithium agreements with Russia and China signed under Arce.<ref name="lithium">{{Cite web |date=26 August 2025 |title=Bolivia candidate vows to scrap China, Russia lithium deals |url=https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20250825-bolivia-candidate-vows-to-scrap-china-russia-lithium-deals |access-date=26 August 2025 |website=France 24}}</ref>
*Distancing Bolivia from Iran and withdrawing recognition of [[Nicolás Maduro]] as president of Venezuela.<ref name="auto"/>

Rodrigo Paz campaigned on a platform of "Capitalism for All",<ref name=Debre19>{{Cite web |first=Isabel |last=DeBre |date=19 August 2025 |title=Bolivia gears up for first-ever presidential runoff, ending years of 1-party rule |url=https://apnews.com/article/bolivia-election-7d24bfd2bc87e4f312d574663aee9f5d |access-date=19 August 2025 |website=AP News}}</ref> which included:

*A "50–50 economic model" where half of public funds would be managed by regional governments.<ref name="reuters"/>
*Cutting $1.2 billion in fuel subsidies while retaining assistance for schoolchildren and the elderly.<ref name="lithium"/><ref>{{Cite web |date=2 September 2025 |title=Presidential hopeful Rodrigo Paz sees 'capitalism for all' as answer to Bolivia's crisis |url=https://apnews.com/article/bolivia-presidential-candidate-paz-lara-morales-f0f1b02befea0e4376f8a47ab4feda5f |access-date=2 September 2025 |website=AP News}}</ref>
*Using [[blockchain]] for transparency and creating a currency stabilization fund that incorporates [[cryptocurrency]].<ref name="reuters"/>
*Opposing Doria Medina's proposals for foreign lithium investment and IMF loans.<ref name=Debre19 />

The MAS candidate, Eduardo del Castillo, campaigned under the slogan "We Are a National Option with Authentic Ideas,"<ref name="apelection"/> while his party colleague Andrónico Rodríguez ran on a platform of "Unity for All" and also supported cutting fuel subsidies.<ref name="apelection"/><ref>{{Cite web |date=11 August 2025 |title=No bread, no fuel, no dollars: how Bolivia went from boom to bust |url=https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20250811-no-bread-no-fuel-no-dollars-how-bolivia-went-from-boom-to-bust |access-date=15 August 2025 |website=France 24}}</ref>

=== Environmental and post-first round dynamics ===
On environmental policy, both Paz and Quiroga supported using carbon bonds. Quiroga advocated for expanding [[biofuel]] production and soy and cattle farming in eastern Bolivia, while Paz pledged to crack down on illegal gold mining and regulate agricultural fires.<ref>{{Cite web |date=21 August 2025 |title=Bolivia will choose a new president but environmental activists see little hope of progress |url=https://apnews.com/article/bolivia-president-election-paz-quiroga-environment-fc2814e4ae4e2ec2fdf24d97a356c990 |access-date=21 August 2025 |website=AP News|first=Steven|last=Grattan}}</ref>

After being barred from the election, Evo Morales called the vote illegitimate, labeled Rodríguez a "traitor", and urged his supporters to cast null ballots. He claimed that if null votes exceeded the winning candidate's share - a situation that eventually did not materialize, as both candidates who advanced to the runoff exceeded the null vote share - it would represent a victory for him.<ref name="guardian"/><ref>{{Cite web |date=14 August 2025 |title=Barred from Bolivia's elections, ex-leader Morales campaigns hard for invalid votes |url=https://apnews.com/article/bolivia-null-vote-election-morales-evo-indigenous-aymara-a1a75b87bbc39c7e143f42a529bb2e54 |access-date=15 August 2025 |website=AP News}}</ref>

Following the first round, Samuel Doria Medina endorsed Rodrigo Paz for the runoff.<ref>{{Cite web |date=18 August 2025 |title=Rodrigo Paz and Tuto Quiroga will compete for the Bolivian presidency in October |url=https://english.elpais.com/international/2025-08-18/rodrigo-paz-and-tuto-quiroga-will-compete-for-the-bolivian-presidency-in-october.html |access-date=18 August 2025 |website=El País}}</ref>

