==Conduct==
Voting for the first round took place on 17 August 2025, with polls open from 08:00 to 16:00.<ref>{{Cite web |date=17 August 2025 |title=Bolivia heads to the polls as 20 years of leftist rule expected to end |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/8/17/bolivia-heads-to-the-polls-as-20-years-of-leftist-rule-expected-to-end |access-date=17 August 2025 |website=Al Jazeera}}</ref>

The day was marked by several security incidents. In [[Chapare Province]], former president Evo Morales cast a null ballot while being protected by a human chain of coca growers' union members, who were shielding him from a potential arrest.<ref name="ap-20250818" />

In [[Entre Ríos, Cochabamba]], Andrónico Rodríguez was targeted by a mob that threw stones at him as he went to vote, requiring a soldier to escort him.<ref>{{Cite web|date=17 August 2025|title=Una turba apedrea al candidato Andrónico Rodríguez tras emitir su voto en Bolivia|url=https://quepasamedia.com/noticias/una-turba-apedrea-al-candidato-andronico-rodriguez-tras-emitir-su-voto-en-bolivia/|access-date=17 August 2025 |website=Qué Pasa Media Network|language=es}}</ref> Rodríguez blamed the attack on "a small group of extremists identified as supporters of Morales."<ref name="result">{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/aug/18/bolivia-presidential-election-preliminary-results |title=Two decades of leftwing dominance end in Bolivia as rightwingers head to election runoff |work=The Guardian|first=Tiago|last=Rogero|date=18 August 2025|access-date=18 August 2025}}</ref> An explosive device was later detonated at the same polling station, though it caused no significant damage or injuries.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c4gzlp85432o |title=Bolivia set to elect first non-left wing president in two decades |work=BBC|first=Ione|last=Wells |date=18 August 2025|access-date=18 August 2025}}</ref>

==Results==
The first-round results ended two decades of left-wing dominance in Bolivia and produced the country's first-ever presidential runoff,<ref name=":5">{{cite news |last1=Sasipornkarn |first1=Emmy |date=18 August 2025 |title=Bolivia's elections headed for first presidential runoff |url=https://www.dw.com/en/bolivias-elections-headed-for-first-presidential-runoff/a-73674414 |access-date=26 August 2025 |work=Deutsche Welle}}</ref> defying pre-election polls.<ref name="ap-20250818" /><ref name="result" /> In a surprise outcome, centrist Rodrigo Paz led with 32.06% of the vote, followed by conservative Jorge Quiroga Ramírez with 26.70%, while Samuel Doria Medina (19.69%) was eliminated.<ref name="result" /> True to his pledge to back the leading candidate if defeated, Doria Medina endorsed Paz for the runoff.<ref>{{cite news |date=17 August 2025 |title=Samuel Medina reconoce su derrota y anuncia respaldo a Rodrigo Paz en la segunda vuelta en Bolivia |url=https://monitoreamos.com/mundo/samuel-medina-reconoce-su-derrota-y-anuncia-respaldo-a-rodrigo-paz-en-la-segunda-vuelta-en-bolivia |access-date=26 August 2025 |work=Monitoreamos}}</ref> Altogether, right-leaning candidates obtained more than 68% of the vote.

The ruling Movimiento al Socialismo (MAS) suffered a historic setback. Its nominees, Andrónico Rodríguez (8.51%) and Eduardo del Castillo (3.17%), failed to reach the second round—marking the party's first presidential loss in twenty years.<ref name=":5" /> MAS was nearly wiped out in Congress, losing all 21 Senate seats and retaining only two of its 75 in the Chamber of Deputies.<ref>{{cite news |date=27 August 2025 |title=Bolivia's long-ruling party was almost eliminated from Congress, final vote results show |url=https://apnews.com/article/bolivia-elections-congress-paz-quiroga-opposition-9d79eeec80bc660b85c243b668b903de |access-date=27 August 2025 |work=AP News}}</ref> The election also saw an unusually high protest vote, with invalid and blank ballots exceeding 20% of the total.<ref>{{cite news |date=18 August 2025 |title=1 in 5 Bolivians spoiled their ballots – a sign of voter dissatisfaction as nation tips to the right |url=https://theconversation.com/1-in-5-bolivians-spoiled-their-ballots-a-sign-of-voter-dissatisfaction-as-nation-tips-to-the-right-263166 |access-date=26 August 2025 |work=The Conversation}}</ref> Former president Evo Morales, who had urged supporters to cast null votes after his exclusion from the race, hailed the result as a symbolic victory.<ref>{{cite news |date=18 August 2025 |title=Présidentielle en Bolivie: l'ancien président Evo Morales salue l'ampleur du vote nul |trans-title=Presidential election in Bolivia: Former president Evo Morales applauds invalid vote numbers |url=https://www.lalibre.be/dernieres-depeches/2025/08/18/presidentielle-en-bolivie-lancien-president-evo-morales-salue-lampleur-du-vote-nul-HKNLWJAKMBAMLO64W7IHGDRWAY/ |access-date=26 August 2025 |work=La Libre |language=fr}}</ref><ref name="result" />

The presidential runoff took place on 19 October 2025.<ref name="runoffresults">{{cite news |last1=Elliot |first1=Lucinda |last2=Machicao |first2=Monica |date=19 October 2025 |title=Bolivian polls close in election steering country closer to US |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/bolivia-votes-runoff-election-marking-pro-market-shift-us-embrace-2025-10-19/ |accessdate=19 October 2025 |publisher=Reuters}}</ref> Preliminary results released after 21:00 local time showed Rodrigo Paz winning with 54.53% of the vote against Jorge Quiroga's 45.47%.<ref name="Reuters Results">{{cite web |last1=Ramos |first1=Daniel |title=Bolivia Centrist Rodrigo Paz wins presidential runoff, early official count shows |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/bolivia-centrist-rodrigo-paz-wins-presidential-runoff-early-official-count-shows-2025-10-20/ |access-date=20 October 2025 |website=[[Reuters]]}}</ref> Quiroga conceded several hours later,<ref name="concedes">{{cite news |date=19 October 2025 |title=Bolivian presidential candidate Jorge 'Tuto' Quiroga concedes election defeat |url=https://ca.news.yahoo.com/bolivian-presidential-candidate-jorge-tuto-023146046.html |work=Yahoo! News}} {{Dead link|date=October 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref> acknowledging Paz's victory and calling on his supporters and the nation to pursue reconciliation and a peaceful transition of power.<ref>{{Cite web |date=20 October 2025 |title=Rodrigo Paz wins presidential runoff, becoming Bolivia's first conservative leader in decades |url=https://apnews.com/article/bolivia-runoff-election-jorge-quiroga-rodrigo-paz-ef7643d07d8968452cdce784e75a7810 |access-date=20 October 2025 |website=[[AP News]]}}</ref>

